---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Solutions"
  description="TBES finds the turbine. If you need assistance, we connect the specialists, including for the control unit."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Solutions"
    title="We find the turbine."
    lede="If you need assistance, we connect the specialists who can put a used unit of any class in shape to run."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="block-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">The work</p>
        <h2>What an engagement can cover</h2>
        <p class="measure">You can use one of these, or move through them in order.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <article class="card">
          <h2>Sourcing and procurement</h2>
          <p>
            We find a used unit that matches the load, any class, in the condition it is in, if specialists can bring it back online. Some new turbine systems are five years out, which is one reason a used machine matters. It is not the only kind of unit we take. We stay with the search until both sides are looking at a real machine.
          </p>
          <p class="eyebrow">You leave with</p>
          <ul class="check-list">
            <li>A duty the search is built around</li>
            <li>Candidate machines, with the offering party named</li>
            <li>A path from offer toward a purchase</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h2>Technical due diligence and equipment verification</h2>
          <p>
            We coordinate a check of condition, history, and nameplate data against the offer. What is missing stays on the list. This is equipment verification. It is not licensed professional engineering, and TurbineBridge is not the engineer of record.
          </p>
          <p class="eyebrow">You leave with</p>
          <ul class="check-list">
            <li>A record of what the offer claimed</li>
            <li>What the check confirmed, and what it did not</li>
            <li>Open items to settle before you commit</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h2>Logistics and transaction management</h2>
          <p>
            We keep the deal and the movement of the machine in one line of work. Parties, documents, and delivery handoffs are coordinated so the transaction stays intact from agreement to the site.
          </p>
          <p class="eyebrow">You leave with</p>
          <ul class="check-list">
            <li>The parties and the scope of the transaction</li>
            <li>Milestones from agreement through delivery</li>
            <li>A single account of what is done and what is open</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h2>Service and rehab teams</h2>
          <p>
            If the customer needs the work bid, we recommend contractors from our network, including shops that handle the control unit. Those companies do the work. We are not the shop, and we are not the engineer of record.
          </p>
          <p class="eyebrow">You leave with</p>
          <ul class="check-list">
            <li>A shop matched to that class of equipment</li>
            <li>A control-unit specialist when the unit needs one</li>
            <li>A path that stays open until the machine can go online</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h2>Advisory on equipment choice</h2>
          <p>
            We advise on equipment choice and power approaches for data centers and industrial facilities. We compare options against the load and the constraints you give us. Your engineer of record stays your engineer of record. We do not practice licensed professional engineering.
          </p>
          <p class="eyebrow">You leave with</p>
          <ul class="check-list">
            <li>Options compared with the load</li>
            <li>Tradeoffs written in plain language</li>
            <li>A recommendation you can take to your own engineers</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="block-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Sequence</p>
        <h2>How an engagement moves</h2>
        <p class="measure">Finding the unit is the work. The check and the transaction follow. Specialist help is there if you need it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step">
            <p class="step-num">01</p>
            <h3>Source</h3>
            <p>We find a used unit of any class that fits the duty and can be brought back online.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <p class="step-num">02</p>
            <h3>Verify</h3>
            <p>We check condition, records, and identity before the buyer relies on the offer.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <p class="step-num">03</p>
            <h3>Transact</h3>
            <p>We coordinate the agreement, the parties, and delivery to the site.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <p class="step-num">04</p>
            <h3>Rehab</h3>
            <p>If you need assistance, we introduce the specialists, including controls. The shops do the work.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="measure">
          The <a class="link" href="/equipment">equipment page</a> shows sample configurations. Availability is confirmed only after an inquiry.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <div class="cta-inner">
      <h2>Start from the duty.</h2>
      <p>Send the load, the machine class, and the timing.</p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact">Send an inquiry</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
